## Non-Negotiable Rules

<CRITICAL_REQUIREMENT type="MANDATORY">

- You MUST NOT change production code, mutation-tool configuration, package manifests, or CI configuration in this skill. Production-code findings are file-pushed to `planning/mt-fix-manifest.md` for follow-up via `bugs.md` in a separate conversation.
- You MAY change test code inline ONLY when the human selects `Action: fix-now` (or `refine`) in Step 4. The inline-fix process (Step 5) mirrors `review-tests.md` Step 2 "Review + Fix Loop": identify the covering test, add the new test or strengthen the assertion, run `{{ "test" | cmd }} <path>` against the modified test file, confirm green, advance to the next cluster.
- You MUST process every cluster as a `Read → think → Edit → next` loop on disk. Conversation context holds AT MOST one cluster's analysis + the file's cached design-section + the file's cached manifest-behaviour. Move to the next cluster ⇒ release the prior cluster from context (it's on disk).
- You MUST emit exactly ONE progress line per file batch — after every cluster in the current file has been processed and the file's outcomes are written. The format is: "File <path>: <N> fix-now / <I> ignored / <B> bugs-pushed + <P> discoveries pushed, <D> discarded (<C> clusters classified; <R> remaining files)". No mid-file progress lines, no prompts in progress lines.
- You MUST present each cluster individually (Step 3) — never batch multiple findings into one prompt. The human confirms or overrides per cluster, mirroring `review-tests.md` §2 "Review + Fix Loop".
- You MUST resolve every real-test-gap cluster as either `fix-now` (inline edit + verify) or `ignore` (rationale recorded; stays in ratchet denominator) or reclassified to `bug`/`equivalent`. There is no defer-to-later for test gaps — the only manifest entries are production bugs.
- You MUST perform per-cluster analysis (read the head's file at `<file:line>`, the design doc, the test manifest) and produce the structured finding (Context, Observable-behaviour analysis, Why-this-matters, Recommendation, Severity, Proposed classification, Proposed action) BEFORE asking the human to confirm. The human confirms or overrides; the human does not re-derive.
- You MUST classify every surviving mutant before Status can move to `triage-complete` — either directly (head) or by inheritance (linked). Skipping is forbidden.
- You MUST honour `split <M-ID>` overrides by removing the listed mutant(s) from their cluster, returning them to the head of the unclassified queue as single-member clusters; each split mutant becomes its own cluster — no re-grouping with other mutants.
- You MUST check the open fix manifest for scope duplicates (Step 2) and propose `link MT-<ID>` instead of creating a new entry when the head's file:line is within ±10 of an existing entry's head AND classification matches.
- You MUST record a one-line rationale for every `ignore` action, every reclassification, and every `equivalent` mutant.
- You MUST surface any cluster head marked `Likely-equivalent: borderline` in Stage 1 (`mt.md` 5.2) — present the borderline flag + 5.2 rationale in Step 3's finding. Human's classification override is the resolution.
- You MUST surface every production bug your per-cluster analysis discovers at a file:line OTHER than the current cluster's mutant. Surface them as `Side findings discovered:` in Step 3 presentation AND log them to the session file's `## Discoveries` section with `Status: logged` as part of per-cluster persistence. Discoveries are triaged per file at Step 6 (push / review / discard), with a defensive sweep at Step 7.5 for any `Status: logged` entries that weren't reviewed. Recording a discovery in conversation reasoning without writing it to the session file is forbidden.
- You MUST run only the exact command `{{ "test" | cmd }} <path>` (test verification after a fix-now inline fix). The path is human-supplied or session-resolved scope data, not an agent-invented flag.
- You MUST write `planning/mt-session.md` to disk after every cluster classification (per-cluster persistence). Per-file write happens at the end of the file batch (Step 6).
- You MUST update the `## Current Batch` pointer at the top of the session file after every file batch (Step 6) — this pointer is the single point of truth for resume. Every resume reads ONLY this pointer + the named file's cluster heads; never the whole `## Surviving Mutants` list.
- You MUST use section-targeted reads — never load the full design doc, full test manifest, or full session file when a named section will do. Step 2 reads only the section covering the current cluster's file on demand.
- You MUST NOT re-read content already in this conversation's context. If a prior cluster in this session loaded a design-doc section or test-manifest behaviour and the current cluster's file is covered by the same section, reuse — do not re-read.
- You MUST update `## Status` at every transition (first classification → triage-in-progress; Step 7 verdict written → triage-complete).
- You MUST NOT invoke `bugs.md`, `write-tests.md`, `fix-tests.md`, or any other framework skill inline. Bug-manifest items are addressed in dedicated conversations later. `fix-now` is handled by this skill itself (test edits) — no skill handoff.
- You MUST NOT use "[enter] to accept" or any keystroke-based convention. Every confirmation prompt uses explicit polarity: `yes` accepts, typing an override changes the proposed value.

</CRITICAL_REQUIREMENT>

